<div class="compare-notes">
  <div class="compare-note">
    <strong>On lock-in.</strong> Project Broadsheet's content is plain Markdown in Git; migrating out is a folder copy. Ghost exports as JSON. Substack gives you a CSV of posts. WordPress gives a database dump. Superdesk exports NewsML XML, which is portable but more complex.
  </div>
  <div class="compare-note">
    <strong>On recurring cost.</strong> Ghost Pro starts at $9/mo for 500 members and scales up. Substack takes 10% of revenue. WordPress adds hosting, plugin, and theme costs. Superdesk has no license fee but requires a multi-service hosting cluster. Project Broadsheet runs on free static hosting.
  </div>
  <div class="compare-note">
    <strong>On who each platform is for.</strong> Substack is built for a single writer with a mailing list. Ghost sits between writer and small team. WordPress does everything for everyone, sometimes poorly. Superdesk is for large newsrooms and wire services. Project Broadsheet is for independent publishers and small teams who want a full editorial experience without the operational overhead.
  </div>
</div>

